Yeah. Two people hear the same song and both go, “Man, remember that summer?” One is back in his brother’s truck. The other is in the kitchen watching her mom get ready for work. They can miss the same summer without having shared a single day of it.

That’s a good example of semantic surface area. The song is only three minutes long, but it touches a different life at a different point for everyone who hears it. And the communal part can come later. You tell me where you were when you heard it, I tell you where I was, and now there’s a little bit of your summer in mine.